@using Edulytics.Web.Curriculum.Grade3
<section class="panel" aria-label="Area and multiplication table representations">
    <h2>Warm-up — Arrays that Grow</h2>
    <p>Look at one arrangement at a time. Think quietly, then tell a partner how many dots you see and how you see them. Compare decompositions into smaller arrays.</p>
    @{
        var arrays=new[] {
            new[] { "1111", "1111" },
            new[] { "0011", "0011", "1111", "1111" },
            new[] { "001100", "001100", "111111", "111111" }
        };
        var descriptions=new[] {
            "Two rows of four dots",
            "Two rows of two dots aligned above the right two columns of two rows of four dots",
            "Two rows of two dots aligned above the middle two columns of two rows of six dots"
        };
    }
    @for(var a=0;a<arrays.Length;a++) {
        <figure><figcaption>Arrangement @(a+1)</figcaption>
            <div role="img" aria-label="@descriptions[a]" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(@(arrays[a][0].Length),22px);gap:5px;width:max-content;margin:12px">
                @foreach(var row in arrays[a]) {
                    @foreach(var dot in row) {
                        <span aria-hidden="true" style="display:block;width:18px;height:18px;border-radius:50%;background:@(dot=='1'?"#183d52":"transparent")"></span>
                    }
                }
            </div>
        </figure>
    }
    <h2>Activity 1 — Area and the Multiplication Table</h2>
    <p>Notice and wonder: compare these four examples. Each rectangle begins at the upper-left data square, excluding the factor headers. Its product is written in its lower-right square.</p>
    @for(var columns=1;columns<=4;columns++) {
        @await Html.PartialAsync("_Grade3MultiplicationTable",new Grade3MultiplicationTable(5,3,columns,true))
    }
    <h3>Your own rectangle</h3>
    <p>Use a copy of this five-by-five table on paper. Create your own rectangle, record its product in its lower-right square, and explain how the factors match the area.</p>
    @await Html.PartialAsync("_Grade3MultiplicationTable",new Grade3MultiplicationTable(5))
    <h3>A rectangle of 24 square units</h3>
    <p>Use this nine-by-nine table. Start at the upper-left DATA square, draw a rectangle of area 24 square units, and record its product in the lower-right square. Both sides must fit the table. Compare a different valid rectangle with a partner.</p>
    @await Html.PartialAsync("_Grade3MultiplicationTable",new Grade3MultiplicationTable(9))
    <h2>Activity 2 — Products in the Multiplication Table</h2>
    <p>Record a notice and a question about the starting table below. Independently find as many other products as you can; start with rows and columns for 2, 5 and 10. You do not have to fill every cell. With the class, complete the rows and columns for 2 and 5. Explain patterns in the row AND column for 5 with your own equations.</p>
    @await Html.PartialAsync("_Grade3MultiplicationTable",new Grade3MultiplicationTable(10,SeedTwos:true))
    <p>Rehearse your explanation with a partner, then share it. Your teacher checks the actual drawings, equations, questions and discussion.</p>
    <h3>Independent practice and new Edulytics Cool-down</h3>
    <p>Use fresh copies of a ten-by-ten table for the separate practice and Cool-down prompts below. Draw each requested rectangle and show your reasoning on paper or in the original-work response field.</p>
    @await Html.PartialAsync("_Grade3MultiplicationTable",new Grade3MultiplicationTable(10))
    <p><a href="https://cms-assets.illustrativemathematics.org/y5sf1a9phst6bjq0u8o441ld0psb" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original 2021 three-page Student Task Statements</a>. These tables and dot arrangements are independently drawn mathematical models. No publisher image files or SVG paths are embedded.</p>
</section>
